"""Authored mathematical developments anchored to explicit historical receipts."""
from pathlib import Path
import json
HERE=Path(__file__).resolve().parent
MONTH='2025-'+HERE.name
entries=[]
def put(n,title,date,evidence,field,source,receipt,question,summary,definitions,result_name,statement,proof,setup,calculation,conclusion,limits,extension,references,origin=None,original_question=None):
 entries.append(dict(id=f'Y25-{HERE.name}-{n:03d}',title=title,status='Developed mathematical result with explicit hypotheses and checked example; historical claims repaired where necessary',summary=summary,definitions=definitions,result=dict(name=result_name,statement=statement,proof=proof),example=dict(setup=setup,calculation=calculation,conclusion=conclusion),limits=limits,extension=extension,references=[dict(label=label,url=url) for label,url in references],original=dict(date=date,month=MONTH,evidence=evidence,field=field,concept=title,origin=origin or 'Recovered dated mathematical topic; newly revised and developed here.',question=question,math=statement,finish='Explicit derivation, worked example and scoped extension',kind='Revised mathematical treatment',refs=[url for label,url in references],source_excerpt=source,retrieval_receipt=receipt,original_question=original_question,attribution_note='The source is a dated retrieval fragment rather than a complete original transcript. Speaker attribution follows the retrieved tag. The revised question, proof and example are newly authored; no missing historical user wording or proof is invented.')))
def save():
 HERE.joinpath('entries.json').write_text(json.dumps(entries,ensure_ascii=False,indent=2))
 print(len(entries))
def src(stamp,filename='root_receipts.json',record=0):
 data=json.loads(HERE.joinpath(filename).read_text())[record]
 lines=[line for c in data['result']['content'] if c.get('type')=='text' for line in c['text'].splitlines()]
 return next(line for line in lines if stamp in line)
HODGE=('Pierre Deligne Théorie de Hodge II','https://doi.org/10.1007/BF02684692')
PERV=('Mark Goresky Lecture notes on sheaves and perverse sheaves','https://arxiv.org/abs/2105.12045')
BBM=('Bender Brody and Müller Hamiltonian for the zeros of the Riemann zeta function','https://arxiv.org/abs/1608.03679')

put(1,'A nilpotent monodromy has a computable weight filtration','2025-01-11','A','Hodge theory',src('2025-01-11T12:12:54Z'),'root_receipts.json record 0',
 'What algebraic data can actually be extracted from a specified unipotent monodromy before adding a quantum correction?',
 'A two-dimensional unipotent matrix supplies an exact logarithm, a centered weight filtration and a compatible sl₂ representation. This concrete calculation isolates the linear algebra used in degeneration theory from an unprovided quantum enrichment.',
 ['Let V=Qe₁⊕Qe₂ and M=[[1,1],[0,1]]. Its nilpotent logarithm is N=logM=M−I because(M−I)²=0.',
 'A monodromy weight filtration centered at one is increasing, satisfiesN(W_k)⊂W_{k−2}, and makesNʲ:Gr^W_{1+j}→Gr^W_{1−j} an isomorphism for every j≥0.'],
 'Explicit monodromy weight calculation',
 'The filtration isW_k=0 fork<0,W₀=W₁=Qe₁,W_k=V fork≥2. The mapN:Gr²_W→Gr⁰_W is an isomorphism. The nontriviality of this filtration does not alone define a limiting mixed Hodge structure.',
 'SinceNe₂=e₁ andNe₁=0, image and kernel ofN are bothQe₁. The proposed filtration is stable underN with the required two-step decrease. Both nonzero graded pieces are one-dimensional and the inducedN maps[e₂] to[e₁], proving the required isomorphism. Higher powers are zero and relate zero graded pieces. For an sl₂ triple setE=N,F=[[0,0],[1,0]],H=diag(1,−1). Multiplication gives[H,E]=2E,[H,F]=−2F,[E,F]=H. Weight index1−H assigns index zero toe₁ and two toe₂, agreeing with the filtration. This is the exact finite representation-theoretic content; a Hodge filtration, polarization and geometric degeneration are additional data.',
 'ApplyM repeatedly and calculate the invariant subspace and coinvariants.',
 'Mⁿ=I+nN=[[1,n],[0,1]] for every integern. Invariants areQe₁; coinvariantsV/NV are generated by[e₂]. They each have dimension one, yet one is a subspace and the other a quotient. Confusing them would erase the nonzero connecting mapN.',
 'The example supplies explicit degeneration linear algebra instead of an undefined list of corrected filtrations.',
 'The computation is not a proof of the geometric monodromy theorem or the Clemens–Schmid sequence. A genuine limiting mixed Hodge structure must satisfy its Hodge and polarization requirements; attaching the word quantum does not provide them.',
 'For a supplied nilpotent matrix, compute its Jordan strings. A lengthr string receives centered weights1−(r−1),1−(r−3),…,1+(r−1). Prove the gradedNʲ isomorphisms string by string and then test compatibility with an independently specified Hodge filtration.',[HODGE])

put(2,'A kernel transform is an equivalence only when it has an inverse','2025-01-11','A','Derived categories',src('2025-01-11T12:12:54Z'),'root_receipts.json record 0',
 'Which explicit kernel operation is an autoequivalence, and what distinguishes that theorem from merely adding a correction term?',
 'On a two-point space the bounded derived category can be classified completely. Equivalences permute the two simple objects and shift them; a shift-and-swap gives an exact involution.',
 ['Letk be a field andD=Dᵇ(Vec_k×Vec_k), represented by pairs of bounded finite-dimensional complexes.',
 'Write[1] for the cohomological shift. Each complex over a field is isomorphic in the derived category to the direct sum of its cohomology spaces placed in their degrees.',
 'The simple objects areS₁=(k,0),S₂=(0,k). Their shifts are the indecomposable objects, and different simples have no morphisms in any degree.'],
 'Classification in a finite derived model',
 'Everyk-linear triangulated equivalence ofD permutesS₁,S₂ and assigns an integer shift to each. In particularF(V,W)=(W[1],V[−1]) is an equivalence withF²≅Id.',
 'Split every differential into its image, a complement in its kernel and a remaining complement; the image/complement pairs form contractible two-term summands. This leaves precisely the graded cohomology, proving the decomposition. An equivalence preserves indecomposability and all graded morphism spaces. It therefore sends eachS_i to a shifted simple. The two images cannot be shifts of the same simple, since then some shifted morphism between them would be nonzero although everyHom(S₁,S₂[n]) vanishes. Essential surjectivity forces both simple types to occur. Conversely a permutation with prescribed shifts has an inverse permutation with the opposite shifts and acts componentwise on morphisms, so it is an equivalence. For the displayedF, applying it twice cancels both shifts.',
 'TakeV=k in degree zero andW=k² in degree zero.',
 'F(V,W) has its two-dimensional first component shifted by one and its one-dimensional second component shifted by minus one. ApplyingF again restores both original complexes exactly up to the standard shift isomorphisms. The operationG(V,W)=(V⊕W,0), by contrast, loses the distinction betweenS₁ andS₂ and has no inverse.',
 'An equivalence is supported by a functor, an inverse and their composition identities, not by the existence of a suggestive kernel notation.',
 'This finite classification is not a Fourier–Mukai theorem for arbitrary varieties. Coherent sheaves on positive-dimensional spaces have extensions and support geometry absent here. The historical quantum-derived categories were not defined sufficiently to reconstruct them.',
 'For a genuine integral transformΦ_K, specify the source/target categories and convolution of kernels, then proveK*L andL*K are diagonal identity kernels. A computable finite model can test these identities before any geometric generalization is claimed.',[('Daniel Huybrechts Fourier–Mukai Transforms in Algebraic Geometry','https://doi.org/10.1093/acprof:oso/9780199296866.001.0001')])

put(3,'Noncommutative observables still have identities and symmetries','2025-01-15','U','Operator algebra',src('2025-01-15T20:37:58Z'),'root_receipts.json record 0',
 'Which algebraic property separates jointly measurable finite observables, without discarding identity operators or symmetry?',
 'Commuting self-adjoint matrices admit a common orthonormal eigenbasis. Noncommuting examples retain an identity and can have exact unitary symmetries; their uncertainty is a quantitative statement about a state.',
 ['An observable in this finite model is a self-adjoint complex matrix. Joint diagonalization means one unitary basis makes every matrix diagonal.',
 'Use PauliX=[[0,1],[1,0]],Z=diag(1,−1),Y=[[0,−i],[i,0]], and identityI.',
 'For normalizedψ, variance is(ΔA)²=||(A−〈A〉)ψ||². A symmetry may act by unitary conjugation.'],
 'Joint diagonalization and a sharp uncertainty example',
 'A finite commuting family of self-adjoint matrices is simultaneously unitarily diagonalizable. Forψ=(1,i)/√2,ΔX=ΔZ=1 and|〈[X,Z]〉|/2=1, attaining the Robertson lower bound.',
 'Diagonalize one self-adjoint matrix. Every matrix commuting with it preserves each eigenspace, becauseA(Bv)=B(Av)=λBv. Restrict the remaining self-adjoint matrices to those invariant eigenspaces and iterate; their orthonormal eigenbases combine into the required basis. Conversely diagonal matrices commute. For the inequality putu=(A−〈A〉)ψ andv=(B−〈B〉)ψ. Cauchy–Schwarz gives||u||||v||≥|Im〈u,v〉|=|〈[A,B]〉|/2. With the statedψ,〈X〉=〈Z〉=0,X²=Z²=I and[X,Z]=−2iY whileYψ=ψ. All claimed numbers follow directly. The same noncommutative algebra still containsI, andXZX=−Z is an exact conjugation symmetry.',
 'CompareI,Z withX,Z and evaluate their products on the chosen state.',
 'I andZ share the standard basis. In contrastXZ=−ZX, so no common eigenbasis exists. NeverthelessI commutes with everything, each Pauli matrix is unitary, and conjugation byX exchanges the twoZ eigenstates. Noncommutativity therefore does not imply absence of identity, symmetry or algebraic consistency.',
 'The original contrast becomes a theorem about simultaneous diagonalization and state-dependent variance.',
 'The finite result does not settle interpretations of quantum measurement or a Platonic ontology. Unbounded observables need common domains; zero commutator expectation in one state does not mean the operators commute.',
 'For an explicit matrix algebra, compute its center and commutant. These identify classical sectors and exact symmetries without assuming that every noncommutative system has the same observable structure.',[('John Preskill Quantum Computation lecture notes','https://www.preskill.caltech.edu/ph219/')])

put(4,'Closing a curve is distinct from choosing a polygon or a winding','2025-01-15','U','Topology',src('2025-01-15T20:37:58Z'),'root_receipts.json record 0',
 'What does returning to the starting point establish, and when can a smooth closed path be replaced by a polygon without changing its winding?',
 'A loop is a continuous map with equal endpoints; it need not be polygonal or noncontractible. A uniformly close polygonal approximation preserves winding whenever the straight-line interpolation avoids the reference point.',
 ['Letγ:[0,1]→C∖{0} be a closed piecewise smooth curve andd=min_t|γ(t)|>0.',
 'Letp be a closed polygonal path withsup_t|p(t)−γ(t)|<d. Winding about zero is the integer degree ofγ/|γ|, or its logarithmic-derivative integral when smooth enough.',
 'A polygon additionally consists of finitely many straight segments. Closure alone supplies neither this regularity nor a nonzero degree.'],
 'A quantitative polygonal replacement theorem',
 'The interpolationH(s,t)=(1−s)γ(t)+sp(t) is a homotopy of closed curves avoiding zero, soγ andp have equal winding. Every continuous nonvanishing loop admits such a polygonal approximation.',
 'The reverse triangle inequality gives|H(s,t)|≥|γ(t)|−s|p(t)−γ(t)|>d−d=0. Endpoints agree for every intermediate curve becauseγ andp are both closed. Homotopy invariance of degree gives equal winding. For existence, uniform continuity provides a partition fine enough thatγ changes by less thanε<d/2 on each subinterval. Linear interpolation between successive sample values then stays within2ε ofγ there; choosing a smallerε gives the required strict bound. This proof explains why polygonal approximation preserves a topological invariant only with a separation margin from the excluded point.',
 'Compare the unit circle, the constant loopγ≡1 and the triangle joining the cube roots of unity.',
 'The circle and triangle each wind once around zero; the constant loop has winding zero although it returns to its start. Refining equal-angle inscribed polygons of the circle makes the uniform interpolation error tend to zero. The strict distance condition prevents an approximating edge from cutting through zero.',
 'Strings, loops and polygons are now distinguished by precise properties rather than by an assumed progression from closure to a particular shape.',
 'Winding is relative to a chosen excluded point. Self-intersections are permitted, and a loop of winding zero need not have a simple image. A path through zero is outside this invariant’s domain.',
 'For a sampled curve with a known Lipschitz constantL and meshh, bound interpolation error byLh and compare it with a certified distance lower boundd. This produces a finite sampling condition for preserving winding under approximation.',[('Allen Hatcher Algebraic Topology','https://pi.math.cornell.edu/~hatcher/AT/ATpage.html')])
put(5,'Boundary conditions are part of a spectral problem','2025-01-15','A','Spectral theory',src('2025-01-15T21:43:20Z'),'root_receipts.json record 0',
 'Which operator domain makes an eigenvalue condition a theorem, and how does changing that domain change the spectrum?',
 'A differential expression alone does not specify an operator. The same second derivative has different spectra under Dirichlet and periodic domains, illustrating the domain gap in a proposed zeta Hamiltonian without pretending to resolve it.',
 ['OnL²(0,π), use the expression−u″. The Dirichlet domain isH²∩H₀¹; the periodic domain requiresu(0)=u(π),u′(0)=u′(π).',
 'An eigenpair consists of a nonzero vector in the chosen domain satisfyingAu=λu. Self-adjointness includes equality with the adjoint domain, not merely a symmetric formal expression.'],
 'Two domains, two exact spectra',
 'The Dirichlet eigenvalues aren² forn≥1, with eigenfunctionssin(nx). The periodic eigenvalues are4n² forn∈Z, including zero, with eigenfunctionse^{2inx}.',
 'Integration by parts gives〈u,−v″〉−〈−u″,v〉=[u′ conjugate(v)−u conjugate(v′)]₀^π, with the precise conjugation placement adjusted to the inner-product convention. Either stated domain kills this boundary form. Solving−u″=λu gives trigonometric solutions forλ>0; Dirichlet endpoints force√λ to be a positive integer. Periodicity forces e^{i√λπ}=1, hence√λ=2|n|. The constant solves the periodic zero eigenvalue but is excluded by Dirichlet endpoints. Completeness follows from the classical sine and Fourier bases. These regular self-adjoint realizations demonstrate how domain information enters both the eigenvalue equation and its spectral interpretation.',
 'Compare the lowest spectral values and boundary behavior.',
 'Dirichlet has lowest eigenvalue one and no constant mode. Periodic has zero followed by four, with the nonzero frequencies occurring in sine/cosine pairs. The same expression therefore gives a different gap and multiplicity structure. A solution of a formal equation that violates an endpoint condition is not an eigenvector of the specified realization.',
 'The repaired question asks for the domain, boundary form and completeness argument before interpreting a spectral list.',
 'The Bender–Brody–Müller proposal is a specific research construction, not a generic synonym for pseudo-Hermitian matrices. Its primary paper explicitly makes the self-adjointness argument conditional. This example proves neither that construction nor the Riemann hypothesis.',
 'For a proposed nonlocal Hamiltonian, state a dense invariant domain, prove closability and characterize the adjoint. Then prove that its boundary map both produces the desired zeros and excludes unwanted spectral points; these are separate implications.',[BBM,('Gerald Teschl Mathematical Methods in Quantum Mechanics','https://www.mat.univie.ac.at/~gerald/ftp/book-schroe/schroe.pdf')])

put(6,'A positive metric can repair a matrix inner product','2025-01-15','A','Linear algebra',src('2025-01-15T21:43:20Z'),'root_receipts.json record 0',
 'When does a non-Hermitian matrix become self-adjoint in a positive inner product, and which obstruction rules this out?',
 'An explicit similarity gives a positive metric and real spectrum for a triangular matrix. A nontrivial Jordan block cannot have such a metric, so pseudo-Hermitian notation alone is insufficient.',
 ['LetA_s=[[1,s],[0,2]] for reals andS=[[1,s],[0,1]], soA_s=Sdiag(1,2)S⁻¹.',
 'A positive metric is a Hermitian positive-definite matrixη defining〈u,v〉_η=u*ηv. Self-adjointness in this inner product isA*η=ηA.'],
 'Explicit quasi-Hermitian metric and its boundary',
 'η_s=[[1,−s],[−s,1+s²]] satisfiesA_s*η_s=η_sA_s and is positive definite. A matrix with a nontrivial Jordan block and real eigenvalue admits no positive metric making it self-adjoint.',
 'Setη=(S⁻¹)*S⁻¹. Thenv*ηv=||S⁻¹v||²>0 for nonzerov. SubstitutingA=SDS⁻¹, withD real diagonal, provesA*η=ηA. For the obstruction, a positiveη has a positive square root. The transformed matrixB=η^{1/2}Aη^{−1/2} satisfiesB*=B, hence is unitarily diagonalizable and has real eigenvalues. Similarity then makesA diagonalizable too. Thus a Jordan defect cannot be hidden in a positive change of inner product. ForJ=[[1,1],[0,1]], writing a real symmetricη=[[a,b],[b,c]] givesJᵀη−ηJ=[[0,−a],[a,0]], forcinga=0 and contradicting positivity. The general similarity argument also covers complex Hermitian metrics.',
 'Takes=1 and compare the two eigenvectors with their ordinary and metric inner products.',
 'Eigenvectorsv₁=(1,0),v₂=(1,1) have ordinary inner product one. Underη=[[1,−1],[−1,2]], their inner product is zero and both have metric norm one. The eigenvalues remain one and two because a change of inner product changes orthogonality, not the algebraic characteristic polynomial.',
 'This is a full finite positive-metric construction with an explicit failure case, rather than a conjectural appeal to spectral reality.',
 'In infinite dimensions, a formal similarity may be unbounded, fail to preserve domains or lack a bounded inverse. The matrix theorem cannot silently be transferred to a proposed zeta Hamiltonian.',
 'Quantify stability through the condition number ofS orη. Even real spectra can have large transient Euclidean amplification in nonnormal coordinates; compare||e^{itA_s}|| with its unit norm in theη metric.',[BBM,('Ali Mostafazadeh Pseudo-Hermiticity versus PT symmetry','https://arxiv.org/abs/math-ph/0107001')])

put(7,'Moonshine coefficients are graded dimensions and traces','2025-01-17','U','Representation theory',src('2025-01-17T08:30:43Z'),'root_receipts.json record 0',
 'What information is contained in a graded character, and how can character arithmetic be verified before attaching a physical interpretation?',
 'A graded trace separates dimension from the action of a symmetry. A smallS₃ representation gives a complete exact decomposition, while the familiar moonshine dimensions are retained as representation-theoretic data.',
 ['For a graded representationV=⊕V_n of a finite group, its character series isTr_V(gq^{L₀})=ΣTr(g|V_n)qⁿ, formally when every graded piece is finite.',
 'Atg=1 the coefficients are dimensions. Away from the identity they are traces, which can differ even for representations of equal dimension.',
 'TheS₃ classes are identity, transpositions and three-cycles, with sizes1,3,2. Its standard character is(2,0,−1).'],
 'An exact tensor-character decomposition',
 'For the two-dimensional standard representationW ofS₃, W⊗W≅1⊕sgn⊕W. At the dimension level the first moonshine coefficient satisfies196884=1+196883; the second satisfies21493760=1+196883+21296876.',
 'The permutation representation on three coordinates splits into the constant line and the plane with coordinate sum zero. Counting fixed coordinates gives its trace(3,1,0), so subtracting the constant line givesW character(2,0,−1). Tensor traces multiply, makingW⊗W character(4,0,1). Summing the characters of1,sgn andW gives(1,1,1)+(1,−1,1)+(2,0,−1)=(4,0,1). Finite-group character orthogonality, proved by averaging matrix coefficients, makes equality of characters equivalent to isomorphism overC. This is a genuine representation calculation; the moonshine dimension sums alone do not prove the existence of a vertex operator algebra or identify physical particle states.',
 'Inspect the tensor trace on a transposition and a three-cycle.',
 'The tensor traces are zero and one, despite the representation having dimension four. The decomposition reproduces both:1−1+0=0 and1+1−1=1. A count of conjugacy classes describes how many irreducible characters exist, not a count of energy levels, measured transitions or spatial directions.',
 'Graded characters provide exact constraints, with their mathematical and observational meanings kept separate.',
 'The Monster moonshine theorem and construction ofV^natural are deep established results, not proved by two integer equalities. The old links to biology, gravity and character-ratio constants are not inherited.',
 'Given a finite graded trace table, compute each irreducible multiplicity by the class-weighted character inner product. Nonintegral or negative multiplicities reject a proposed representation immediately; passing these tests still does not supply its multiplication or VOA axioms.',[('Richard Borcherds Monstrous moonshine and monstrous Lie superalgebras','https://doi.org/10.1007/BF01232032')])

put(8,'Modular invariance has a concrete weight and coefficient test','2025-01-17','U','Modular forms',src('2025-01-17T08:30:43Z'),'root_receipts.json record 0',
 'How do theE₄ and discriminant expansions producej, and what modular-invariance statement follows from their weights?',
 'The classical quotientj=E₄³/Δ has weight zero. Formal division recovers its first coefficients exactly, providing arithmetic content without deriving a conformal field theory from a numerical coincidence.',
 ['Useq=e^{2πiτ}, E₄=1+240q+2160q²+6720q³+…, andΔ=q−24q²+252q³−1472q⁴+….',
 'A weightk modular form transforms byf((aτ+b)/(cτ+d))=(cτ+d)^k f(τ), for matrices inSL₂(Z). E₄ has weight four andΔ weight twelve.',
 'The normalized moonshine series isJ=j−744, so a constant shift distinguishesj fromJ.'],
 'Weight-zero quotient and exact initial Fourier coefficients',
 'WhereΔ≠0, j transforms invariantly underSL₂(Z). Its expansion beginsq⁻¹+744+196884q+21493760q². ConsequentlyJ has zero constant term and the same positive-power coefficients.',
 'The transformation factors ofE₄³ andΔ both have exponent twelve, so their quotient factors cancel. This gives the invariance directly from the stated modular-form theorem. For the arithmetic calculation, E₄³=1+720q+179280q²+16954560q³+…. Writej=q⁻¹+c₀+c₁q+c₂q²+… and multiply byΔ. Matchingq terms givesc₀−24=720, soc₀=744. Matchingq² givesc₁−24·744+252=179280, yielding196884. Matchingq³ givesc₂−24·196884+252·744−1472=16954560, yielding21493760. Every step uses finite exact coefficient arithmetic; no floating-point fitting is involved.',
 'Comparej andJ and test the basic translationτ↦τ+1.',
 'Translation leavesq unchanged, so each displayed Fourier series is invariant under that generator. Inversionτ↦−1/τ follows from the weight cancellation, not from a finite truncation. Subtracting744 changes only the graded constant coefficient, which matters when matching a proposed state-space character.',
 'The result explains exactly which modular statement is proved and which coefficients are certified.',
 'A finiteq-series match is not a proof of a global modular identity unless a theorem such as a dimension or Sturm bound supplies the upgrade. Modular invariance alone does not construct a positive Hilbert space, OPE or physical measurement model.',
 'For a proposed character vector, state its representation ofSL₂(Z), transformation law and pole orders. Then use an appropriate finite-dimensional modular-form bound to determine whether a certified finite coefficient comparison proves an identity.',[('J S Milne Modular Functions and Modular Forms','https://www.jmilne.org/math/CourseNotes/MF.pdf')])

put(9,'Projective phases require a genuine two-cocycle','2025-01-18','U','Group cohomology',src('2025-01-18T17:13:26Z'),'root_receipts.json record 0',
 'Which phase factors define an associative projective representation, and how can one prove that they cannot be removed by rephasing?',
 'A two-cocycle onC₂×C₂ has an explicit Pauli realization and a nontrivial commutator phase. This supplies a rigorous model of projective symmetry instead of assuming a claimed Monster cohomology value.',
 ['LetG=F₂² under addition andσ((a,b),(c,d))=(−1)^{bc}. DefineU_(a,b)=X^aZ^b using the Pauli matrices.',
 'A normalized two-cocycle satisfiesσ(g,h)σ(g+h,k)=σ(h,k)σ(g,h+k) andσ(0,g)=σ(g,0)=1.',
 'RephasingU_g by a nonzero scalar changesσ by a coboundary. On an abelian group every scalar coboundary has symmetric values in its two arguments.'],
 'A nontrivial projective representation with an exact certificate',
 'U_gU_h=σ(g,h)U_(g+h), andσ is a two-cocycle that is not a coboundary. Thus the projective action cannot be made an ordinary representation by choosing new scalar phases.',
 'The relationZX=−XZ gives the displayed multiplier when movingZ^b pastX^c. For triple elementsg=(a,b),h=(c,d),k=(e,f), the cocycle exponents arebc+(b+d)e andde+b(c+e), equal modulo two. This proves associativity of the central-extension multiplication. For the obstruction chooseg=(1,0),h=(0,1). Their multiplier ratioσ(g,h)/σ(h,g)=−1. Replacingσ by a coboundary cannot change that ratio because the group is abelian and a coboundaryc(g)c(h)/c(g+h) is symmetric. Hence the class is nonzero. The eight matrices{±I,±X,±Z,±XZ} form the corresponding noncommutative central extension.',
 'Compute the commutator of the lifts of the two order-two generators.',
 'BothX andZ square toI, butXZX⁻¹Z⁻¹=−I and(XZ)²=−I. Their images commute after quotienting by{±I}, exactly recoveringG. The lift group has order eight, while its quotient has four elements. Orbit–stabilizer likewise requires a specified action; the order of an ambient group alone does not determine orbit sizes.',
 'A central phase now has a checked algebraic origin and a concrete obstruction to removal.',
 'This example concernsG=F₂². It does not establish the historical assertionH²(M,C*)=Z/2 for the Monster; a group-specific Schur-multiplier calculation must be cited separately. No continuous spacetime symmetry follows from this finite extension.',
 'Given a proposed finite multiplier table, test the cocycle identity exhaustively, compute its alternating commutator form and solve the rephasing equations. This separates a removable convention from a nontrivial projective class.',[('J S Milne Group Theory','https://www.jmilne.org/math/CourseNotes/GT.pdf')])
put(10,'Six pentads give an exhaustive outer automorphism certificate','2025-01-21','A','Finite group combinatorics',src('2025-01-21T00:14:05Z'),'root_receipts.json record 0',
 'Can the exceptional symmetry ofS₆ be exhibited by a finite construction rather than inferred from an analogy with particle generations?',
 'Perfect matchings ofK₆ form fifteen synthemes and six complete factorizations called pentads. Permuting the six original vertices acts on these six pentads and gives an outer automorphism, with all720 images independently enumerable.',
 ['A duad is an unordered pair of six labels. A syntheme is a partition of those labels into three duads, equivalently a perfect matching ofK₆.',
 'A pentad is a set of five synthemes whose fifteen edges are pairwise distinct, hence cover all edges ofK₆ exactly once.',
 'An inner automorphism ofS₆ is conjugation by a permutation and therefore preserves cycle type.'],
 'Explicit exceptional permutation action',
 'There are fifteen synthemes and six pentads. The actionS₆→Sym(pentads) is bijective. A vertex transposition acts on pentads as three disjoint transpositions, so this automorphism is outer.',
 'Construct a matching by pairing the least unused vertex with each remaining vertex and recursing; there are5·3·1=15 outcomes. Enumerate all five-subsets of these fifteen matchings and retain exactly those whose edge union has size fifteen. This finite exhaustive procedure yields six pentads, retained in the verification certificate. Relabeling vertices preserves disjointness and coverage, so it permutes the six retained objects and defines a homomorphism. Enumerating all720 vertex permutations yields720 distinct permutations of the pentads, proving injectivity and, since the target also has order720, surjectivity. The image of a transposition has no fixed pentad and order two, so its cycle type is2³. Conjugation cannot take cycle type2·1⁴ to2³, proving outerness. The construction and exhaustive checks together are a finite proof certificate; the source supplies no physical identification for this automorphism.',
 'Apply the label swap(0 1) to each of the six certified pentads.',
 'The pentads pair into three exchanged pairs. Applying the swap twice restores every matching, but one application fixes none of the six pentads. This contrasts with its four fixed original labels and makes the change of cycle type directly visible.',
 'The exceptional automorphism is now fully specified by finite combinatorial data and a complete enumeration.',
 'The numbering of the six pentads chooses a particular automorphism; different numberings differ by an inner automorphism. This does not identify particle families, Monster representations or a continuous gauge group.',
 'Construct the induced permutation on conjugacy classes and check that transpositions exchange with triple transpositions. Then restrict toA₆ and distinguish inner automorphisms ofA₆ from those induced by odd permutations ofS₆.',[('Noam Elkies Algebra notes on the outer automorphism','https://people.math.harvard.edu/~elkies/M155.15/notes.html')])

put(11,'An E₈ projection has exact fibers and changes root lengths','2025-01-28','A','Root systems',src('2025-01-28T22:02:41Z'),'root_receipts.json record 0',
 'What does a specifiedE₈-to-four-coordinate projection actually produce, including multiplicities and zero fibers?',
 'Projecting the standard240 E₈roots onto their first four coordinates gives the48 F₄root vectors with unequal multiplicities, together with zero. The24 long projected roots formD₄, but the entire image is larger.',
 ['TheE₈roots are112 vectors±e_i±e_j fori<j and128 half-integral vectors(±1/2,…,±1/2) with an even number of minus signs.',
 'Letπ:R⁸→R⁴ retain the first four coordinates. Root length is measured with the standard Euclidean inner product.',
 'TheD₄roots are the24 vectors±e_i±e_j in four coordinates. F₄ adds the eight vectors±e_i and sixteen half-sign vectors.'],
 'Complete projected-root fiber distribution',
 'The24 D₄vectors each have one preimage. Each of the24 shortF₄vectors has eight preimages. Zero has24 preimages. Thus24+24·8+24=240, and squared projected lengths are2,1 and0.',
 'Integral roots supported among the first four coordinates give6·4=24 D₄vectors. Those supported among the last four give24 zero images. Mixed support selects one of four visible and one of four hidden coordinates and two signs, giving64 roots: for each visible±e_i there are four hidden-coordinate choices and two hidden signs, hence eight preimages. For a fixed visible half-sign pattern, the last four signs must have a prescribed parity. Exactly eight of their sixteen patterns satisfy it, producing128 half-root images with multiplicity eight. This exhausts both original root types. The length assertions follow by summing coordinate squares and explicitly show why this projection is not an isometry or a bijection of root systems.',
 'Track one long root, one short image and the zero fiber.',
 'e₁+e₂ maps to aD₄root of squared length two. The eight vectors e₁±e_j forj=5,…,8 all map toe₁ of squared length one. Every±e_i±e_j with5≤i<j≤8 maps to zero. The verifier constructs all240 roots as integer doubled coordinates and records the complete fiber histogram without rounding.',
 'AnE₈→D₄ slogan becomes a precise linear map with a complete accounting of what it preserves and collapses.',
 'The projection does not by itself define a Lie-algebra homomorphismE₈→D₄ or a branching rule for arbitrary representations. Root-image multiplicities are not particle masses or coupling constants.',
 'Compare this root projection with restriction of the adjoint representation to an explicitly embeddedD₄subalgebra. Compute its actual weight multiplicities, including Cartan zero weights, rather than treating projected roots as a complete representation decomposition.',[('J S Milne Lie Algebras Algebraic Groups and Lie Groups','https://www.jmilne.org/math/CourseNotes/LAG.pdf')])

put(12,'Platonic boundaries share homology but have different symmetry groups','2025-01-24','A','Algebraic topology and polyhedra',src('2025-01-24T14:58:50Z'),'root_receipts.json record 0',
 'Which topology remains unchanged between a sphere and a convex polyhedral boundary, and which symmetry data can distinguish the Platonic cases?',
 'Radial projection identifies every convex polyhedral boundary surrounding the origin withS². Its homology is therefore the same across the Platonic solids, while face counts and rotational group orders distinguish their geometry.',
 ['LetP⊂R³ be a convex polytope containing zero in its interior. Every ray meets∂P in exactly one point.',
 'Use homology over a field. The boundary is a two-dimensional cell complex; including the filled interior changes the space to a contractible ball.',
 'For a regular solid withF regularp-gon faces, rotations act transitively on faces and a face stabilizer has orderp.'],
 'Topological invariance and finite symmetry counts',
 '∂P is homeomorphic toS² and has Betti numbers(1,0,1). The tetrahedron,cube,octahedron,dodecahedron,icosahedron have(V,E,F) respectively(4,6,4),(8,12,6),(6,12,8),(20,30,12),(12,30,20), with rotational group orders12,24,24,60,60.',
 'Map a boundary pointx tox/||x||. Convexity and the interior-origin condition give a unique positive intersection on every ray, so this map is bijective. It is continuous from a compact space to the Hausdorff sphere, hence a homeomorphism. The classical homology ofS² gives the Betti numbers. For the listed regular solids, each face hasp edges and every edge bounds two faces, soFp=2E. Their Euler countsV−E+F=2 agree with the topological result. Orbit–stabilizer gives rotation orderFp: a face can be sent to any ofF faces, withp rotations preserving its center and cyclically permuting its vertices. In the tetrahedral case, the induced even vertex permutations realizeA₄; cube/octahedron rotations realizeS₄, and the remaining pair realizesA₅.',
 'Build the tetrahedron boundary chain complex and compare it with the filled tetrahedron.',
 'The boundary has chain dimensions4,6,4 and boundary ranks3,3, giving Betti numbers1,0,1. Adding the single3-cell has nonzero boundary equal to the oriented sum of the four faces, killingH₂ and leaving onlyH₀. This is an exact certificate of the difference between boundary and filling.',
 'Homology alone cannot distinguish the five regular boundaries, but the underlying cell and group structures can.',
 'The historical22-equation list is truncated; its missing members are not reconstructed. The argument requires convexity and an interior reference point; an arbitrary singular wave level set need not be a sphere.',
 'For an explicitly supplied nonconvex or singular mesh, verify its boundary maps and manifold links before using Euler characteristic as a topology classifier. Add the actual symmetry action as separate data when homology is insufficient.',[('Allen Hatcher Algebraic Topology','https://pi.math.cornell.edu/~hatcher/AT/ATpage.html')])

put(13,'A scalar wave potential has critical points, not a square Jacobian','2025-01-24','A','Differential topology',src('2025-01-24T20:03:25Z'),'root_receipts.json record 0',
 'Which derivative detects a singular level of a scalar wave field, and how is that different from the Jacobian determinant of a vector map?',
 'A scalar field onR³ has a gradient and Hessian. A determinant belongs to a square derivative of a declared three-component map. Two explicit examples distinguish critical values, Morse indices and fold singularities.',
 ['ForΦ:R³→R, a levelΦ=c is regular when∇Φ≠0 at every point on that level.',
 'ForF:R³→R³, its derivative is3×3 anddetDF detects rank loss. These are different maps and cannot be denoted by an untyped determinant of one scalar.',
 'OnT³ useΦ(x,y,z)=cosx+cosy+cosz, with coordinates modulo2π.'],
 'Complete critical-point calculation and fold model',
 'The potential has eight critical points, each coordinate zero orπ. Its critical values are3,1,−1,−3 and all eight points are Morse. The vector mapF(x,y,z)=(x²,y,z) has singular plane{x=0} and critical-value plane{u=0}.',
 'The gradient is(−sinx,−siny,−sinz), so it vanishes precisely at the eight listed points. The Hessian isdiag(−cosx,−cosy,−cosz), with every diagonal entry±1 there, so its determinant is nonzero. A zero coordinate contributes a negative eigenvalue; the index counts the number of zero coordinates. There arebinomial(3,k) points of indexk. Their alternating count1−3+3−1=0 matchesχ(T³). For the vector fold, DF=diag(2x,1,1), so rank drops exactly atx=0. An image point(u,v,w) has two preimages foru>0, one foru=0 and none foru<0. This is a complete local multiplicity change, obtained from a correctly typed determinant.',
 'Compare the maximum ofΦ at(0,0,0) with a point of the fold plane.',
 'The potential maximum has value three and Hessian−I, so its scalar level becomes singular there. The fold map instead has derivative rank two at(0,v,w), with a one-dimensional kernel in thex direction. The two tests answer different questions even though both involve a loss of regular behavior.',
 'Wave geometry is now expressed through explicit functions and regularity hypotheses rather than an undefined singular-locus symbol.',
 'The chosen cosine field is a new tractable example, not a recovered experimental reconstruction. Morse counts do not identify a derived stack, intersection complex or a sound-driven geometry.',
 'For a supplied finite wave sum, compute its gradient and Hessian symbolically, isolate all critical points and certify the Hessian signs. For a vector-valued measurement map, instead study its rank strata and the stability of inverse branches.',[('John Milnor Morse Theory','https://press.princeton.edu/books/paperback/9780691080086/morse-theory')])

put(14,'Intersection homology retains cycles that an ordinary cone contracts','2025-01-24','A','Intersection homology',src('2025-01-24T14:58:50Z'),'root_receipts.json record 0',
 'What does a perversity allow near a singular point, and can it change the homology of a contractible cone?',
 'The open cone on a torus is contractible in ordinary topology, yet lower-middle intersection homology retains two first-homology classes. The discrepancy comes from which bounding chains may touch the cone point.',
 ['LetL=T² andX=cL be its open real three-dimensional cone, with the apex as a codimension-three singular stratum.',
 'Use field coefficients and lower-middle perversity p(3)=0. An allowablei-chain may meet the apex only in dimension at mosti−3; its boundary must satisfy the analogous condition.',
 'This is a fixed stratified pseudomanifold and perversity, not an unspecified objectIC(X).'],
 'Explicit cone-formula instance',
 'IH₀(X)=k,IH₁(X)=k² andIH_i(X)=0 fori≥2 under the stated perversity. OrdinaryH₁(X)=0 because the whole cone contracts.',
 'Allowable chains in dimensions zero,one and two cannot meet the apex: their permitted intersection dimensions are negative. Thus first cycles and their allowable bounding two-chains lie inX∖{apex}=T²×(0,∞), which retracts ontoT². Its two independent one-cycles therefore survive inIH₁. A two-cycle in the link can be coned to an allowable three-chain, because a three-chain may meet the apex at a zero-dimensional set. This kills the link’s fundamental two-cycle. More generally the standard cone contraction applies above the cutoff; the cone formula statesIH_i(cL)=IH_i(L) fori<dimL−p(3)=2 and zero above. The chain explanation proves the low-degree distinction directly and identifies why an ordinary apex-crossing disk is inadmissible.',
 'Take the two coordinate circles ofT² at a fixed cone radius.',
 'They generate a two-dimensionalIH₁. In ordinary homology each bounds its cone disk, but that two-chain meets the apex and violates2−3<0. The link’s two-dimensional fundamental cycle does bound its three-dimensional cone, where3−3=0 allows the vertex. The verifier checks the torus chain ranks and ordinary cone contraction as finite supporting calculations.',
 'Perversity controls allowable fillings, so contractibility does not force this refined homology to vanish.',
 'Changing to upper-middle perversity p(3)=1 changes the cutoff and removes the retainedH₁. Shifts and indexing of an intersection complex must also be specified before equating its hypercohomology to anIH group.',
 'Construct an explicit triangulated link and its cone, label singular strata, and implement allowable-chain restrictions degree by degree. Compare the resulting homology with the stated cone formula before using it in a larger singular space.',[('Goresky and MacPherson Intersection homology theory','https://doi.org/10.1016/0040-9383(80)90003-8')])
put(15,'The derived tangent complex comes from the cotangent complex','2025-01-26','A','Derived geometry',src('2025-01-26T06:53:41Z'),'root_receipts.json record 0',
 'Which complex captures infinitesimal deformations and obstructions at a singular point?',
 'For a hypersurface the cotangent complex is an explicit two-term derivative map. At a double point it detects both a tangent direction and an obstruction, information not supplied byRHom(O_X,O_X).',
 ['Over a characteristic-zero fieldk letA=k[x]/(x²), with pointx=0.',
 'For a hypersurface the cotangent complex is[I/I²→Ω_{k[x]/k}⊗A] in degrees−1,0. Here it is[A→A] with differential multiplication by2x.',
 'The tangent complex at the point isRHom_A(L_{A/k},k), with cohomology in degrees zero and one.'],
 'Double-point deformation and obstruction',
 'Atx=0 the tangent-complex differential is zero, soH⁰≅k andH¹≅k. Every first-order displacementx=at satisfiesx²=0 modulot², but it extends tox=at+bt² modulot³ only ifa²=0.',
 'The relation generatorx² differentiates to2x dx, giving the two-term cotangent map. Tensoring with the residue field killsx, and dualizing givesk→k with zero differential in degrees0,1. For an explicit deformation, substituteat into the defining relation overk[t]/t²: its square vanishes automatically. Overk[t]/t³, the square ofat+bt² isa²t², so extension requiresa²=0. For a field this forcesa=0, exhibiting the nonlinear obstruction to a nonzero first-order tangent direction. The complex predicts a possible obstruction space; the equation computes the actual obstruction mapa↦a². By contrastRHom_A(A,A)=A in degree zero becauseA is a free module over itself, so it misses this deformation structure.',
 'Compare with the smooth hypersurfacek[x]/(x), whose derivative map is multiplication by one.',
 'The smooth one-point scheme has zero tangent complex: the mapk→k is an isomorphism. The double point has the same underlying topological point but nontrivial tangent and obstruction spaces. Thus topology of the underlying set cannot recover its derived infinitesimal geometry.',
 'The correction supplies a concrete deformation problem and an actual failed lift, not only a replacement symbol.',
 'The displayed hypersurface model uses a regular-sequence presentation. General derived schemes require the full cotangent complex and derived base change. A nonzero obstruction space does not mean every deformation is obstructed.',
 'Forf₁,…,f_r forming a regular sequence, compute the Jacobian mapkⁿ→kʳ at a point. Its kernel and cokernel give tangent and first obstruction spaces; then compute higher-order lift equations to distinguish a potential obstruction from a nonzero one.',[('Stacks Project The cotangent complex','https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/08P5')])

put(16,'Perverse degree is fixed by support and cosupport','2025-01-26','A','Perverse sheaves',src('2025-01-26T06:53:41Z'),'root_receipts.json record 0',
 'Which degree shift makes a local system perverse on a smooth complex curve, and what changes for a point-supported object?',
 'Middle perversity is a pair of support conditions, not a freely adjustable label. On a smooth complex curve a local system shifted by one is perverse, while a skyscraper is perverse in degree zero.',
 ['Work with bounded constructible complexes of finite-dimensionalk-vector spaces on a smooth complex algebraic curve C.',
 'Use the middle-perverse criteriondim_C supp H^i(K)≤−i and the same inequalities for Verdier dualD K.',
 'For a smooth oriented real two-manifoldD(k_C)=k_C[2]; for a point-supported finite-dimensional sheaf, Verdier duality takes its vector-space dual without a shift.'],
 'Curve and point perverse objects',
 'A finite-rank local systemL[1] is perverse onC. A skyscraperk_p is perverse. The unshifted nonzero local systemL is not perverse.',
 'The only nonzero ordinary cohomology sheaf ofL[1] lies in degree−1 and has one-dimensional support, exactly satisfying1≤−(−1). Its Verdier dual isL^∨[1], which satisfies the same inequality. A skyscraper has degree-zero cohomology supported in dimension zero and is self-dual up to vector-space dual, so both inequalities hold. The unshiftedL instead has degree-zero support of dimension one, violating1≤0. This computation checks both halves of the definition and demonstrates why ordinary cohomological degree and perverse degree differ. Direct sums of these two types remain perverse because support dimensions take maxima and duality distributes over finite sums.',
 'TakeK=k_C[1]⊕k_p and compute its ordinary cohomology sheaves.',
 'H⁻¹(K)=k_C andH⁰(K)=k_p, with support dimensions one and zero. Both are allowed simultaneously. Moving the skyscraper to degree one would give a nonzero degree-one sheaf with support dimension zero, violating0≤−1. Thus even a tiny complex has a sharply constrained perverse grading.',
 'The repaired formulation gives a testable definition and two explicit objects rather than assuming a quantum perverse category exists.',
 'The general theory requires constructibility, duality and a chosen perversity. An extra parameterq must come with a defined category or functor and a proof of compatibility; the recovered symbolP_q(T) does not supply those data.',
 'For an open inclusionj:C∖{p}→C, comparej_!L[1],Rj_*L[1] and the intermediate extensionj_!*L[1]. Compute local monodromy invariants and coinvariants to identify which point-supported subobjects or quotients are removed.',[PERV])

put(17,'A persistence module retains finite bars that its colimit forgets','2025-01-26','A','Persistent homology',src('2025-01-26T06:53:41Z'),'root_receipts.json record 0',
 'Which object records birth and death times, and why is a terminal colimit not an adequate definition of persistence?',
 'A finite triangle filtration has a one-cycle born at time two and killed at time three. ItsH₁ colimit is zero despite a nontrivial persistent interval.',
 ['LetX₀ contain three vertices. At time one add edgesAB andBC; at time two addAC; at time three fill the triangleABC.',
 'Persistent homology is the vector-space diagramt↦H_i(X_t;k) together with maps induced by inclusions.',
 'An interval modulek[b,d) has a one-dimensional space for b≤t<d, zero otherwise, and identity maps within its lifetime.'],
 'Complete barcode of the triangle filtration',
 'H₀ consists of one interval[0,∞) and two intervals[0,1). H₁ consists of[2,3). Higher homology vanishes. The colimitH₁ is zero, while the rank ofH₁(X₂)→H₁(X₂) is one.',
 'At time zero all three vertices are separate, soH₀ has dimension three. The two added edges form a tree, whose incidence matrix has rank two, merging all components without creatingH₁. The third edge raises the edge count to three while the incidence rank stays two, producing one cycle. Adding the face gives a nonzero boundary equal to that cycle, so it dies at time three. All inclusion maps follow directly from these chain generators. The interval decomposition is therefore explicit rather than merely asserted. The directed colimit identifies every vector with its later image; the cycle maps to zero after the filling and disappears. It records the eventual space but not how long a transient feature existed.',
 'Write the three edge boundaries in the vertex basis and the face boundary in the edge basis.',
 'Use∂AB=B−A,∂BC=C−B,∂AC=C−A, so the cycleAB+BC−AC has zero boundary. The face boundary is exactlyAB+BC−AC. Exact rank calculation gives Betti sequencesH₀:(3,1,1,1),H₁:(0,0,1,0).',
 'The reconstructed definition preserves the actual diagram and its maps; replacing it bycolim_tH^i loses the feature this example is designed to detect.',
 'Interval decomposition here uses a one-parameter finite filtration over a field. Multi-parameter persistence has no general barcode classification by intervals. The motivic adjective requires additional coefficient and functorial data.',
 'For any supplied filtration, retain the rank invariant rank(H_i(X_s)→H_i(X_t)) as a certificate. Test whether a proposed simplification preserves these ranks, rather than comparing only the final Betti numbers.',[('Edelsbrunner Letscher and Zomorodian Topological persistence and simplification','https://doi.org/10.1007/s00454-002-2885-2')])

put(18,'A weight filtration need not split canonically','2025-01-26','A','Filtered linear algebra',src('2025-01-26T06:53:41Z'),'root_receipts.json record 0',
 'What is the difference between a filtration, its associated graded object and a chosen direct-sum splitting?',
 'A two-step filtered vector space admits many splittings, none invariant under all filtration-preserving automorphisms. This elementary example identifies the extra choice suppressed by writing a weight filtration as a direct sum.',
 ['LetV=ke₁⊕ke₂,W₀=ke₁,W₁=W₀,W₂=V, with the evident zero and full extensions outside this range.',
 'The associated graded isGr⁰_W=ke₁ andGr²_W=V/ke₁. A splitting selects a lift of each quotient vector and identifiesV with their direct sum.',
 'A canonical splitting would have to be preserved by every automorphism of the given filtered object.'],
 'No functorial splitting for this filtered object',
 'Every complement ofW₀ has the formk(e₂+ce₁). The filtered automorphismsT_a(e₁)=e₁,T_a(e₂)=e₂+ae₁ act transitively on these complements. No complement is fixed by allT_a.',
 'A line complementary toke₁ has a vector with nonzeroe₂ coordinate; rescaling it gives e₂+ce₁, proving the classification. ApplyingT_a sends this line to the line with parameterc+a. Choosing any nonzeroa changes it, so no complement can be invariant under the whole automorphism group. EachT_a induces the identity on both graded quotients, showing that the associated graded forgets genuine extension automorphisms. The equality of graded dimensions withdimV therefore does not provide a distinguished decomposition. In mixed Hodge theory additional structures constrain the extensions, and certain splittings may exist after changing scalars or adding hypotheses; those theorems must be stated rather than replacingW_k by an unexplained Hodge-index sum.',
 'Compare the complementske₂ andk(e₂+3e₁).',
 'Both project isomorphically toGr²_W and yield identical graded dimensions(1,1). T₃ exchanges the first with the second while acting trivially onGr_W. A numerical routine that returns one complement has selected coordinates, not found an invariant hidden direct-sum decomposition.',
 'The finite calculation explains exactly why filtrations and decompositions encode different information.',
 'This is a filtered-vector-space theorem, not a classification of mixed Hodge extensions. A pure Hodge decomposition is a different structure and can be canonical under its own hypotheses. The historical formulaW_k=⊕_{p+q=k}H_M^{p,q} conflates indices unless its objects are redefined.',
 'Add a specified nondegenerate inner product and take the orthogonal complement. Then identify the smaller symmetry group preserving that metric and show the splitting is natural only for that smaller category. This exposes the cost of the additional choice.',[HODGE])

put(19,'Localization gives a valid motivic and cohomological subtraction','2025-01-26','A','Motivic and sheaf-theoretic additivity',src('2025-01-26T06:53:41Z'),'root_receipts.json record 0',
 'Which additivity law underlies a motivic expression, and how does a concrete open/closed decomposition act on cohomology?',
 'The projective line minus two points gives a fully specified localization example. It has classL−1 and compactly supported cohomology in degrees one and two; no undefined infinite motivic series is needed.',
 ['In the Grothendieck ring of varieties,[X]=[Z]+[X∖Z] for a closed subvarietyZ. SetL=[A¹].',
 'LetX=P¹_C,Z={0,∞},U=C*. The open/closed trianglej_!k_U→k_X→i_*k_Z produces a long exact compact-support cohomology sequence.',
 'The existence of this sheaf-theoretic localization sequence is standard background; its maps and dimensions are computed below.'],
 'Exact class and compact-support cohomology ofC*',
 '[C*]=L−1. Over a fieldk,H_c¹(C*;k)=k,H_c²(C*;k)=k and the other compact-support groups vanish. Its compact-support Euler characteristic is zero.',
 'DecomposeP¹ intoA¹ and the point at infinity to obtain[P¹]=L+1. Removing two points givesL−1. In the long exact sequence, H⁰(P¹)=k maps toH⁰(Z)=k² by the diagonal mapc↦(c,c). Its kernel is zero and cokernel is one-dimensional, soH_c⁰(U)=0 andH_c¹(U)=k. SinceH¹(P¹)=0,H¹(Z)=0,H²(P¹)=k andH²(Z)=0, exactness givesH_c²(U)=k. Hence the Euler characteristic is−1+1=0. The cohomological subtraction includes connecting maps and degrees; it is not literal subtraction of ordinary vector spaces. Its numerical Euler realization agrees with evaluatingL at one in the classL−1.',
 'Write the finite matrix for the only nontrivial map in low degrees.',
 'The column(1,1)ᵀ has rank one. The quotientk²/diag(k) is detected by(a,b)↦b−a. Thus two removed points create one compact-support degree-one class, even thoughC* itself has no compact connected component.',
 'This is a constructive interpretation of additivity, six-operation notation and a motivic class in one exact example.',
 'A motivic integral over arcs additionally needs a measure, cylinder normalization and a completed coefficient ring. The recovered sumΣ[X_n]L^−n does not automatically converge or define that measure. No quantum volume is inferred.',
 'ForP¹ withr punctures, repeat the same diagonal-map calculation to obtainH_c¹ dimensionr−1 andH_c² dimension one, and verify the classL+1−r. Then state what additional completion would be needed for an actual arc-space sum.',[('Stacks Project Grothendieck groups of varieties','https://stacks.math.columbia.edu'),PERV])

put(20,'A Jacobiator detects nonassociativity only after multiplication is defined','2025-01-28','A','Nonassociative algebra',src('2025-01-28T22:02:41Z'),'root_receipts.json record 0',
 'Can a claimed nonzero flux Jacobiator be distinguished from the identically zero Jacobiator of associative operator commutators?',
 'Associative commutators satisfy Jacobi by cancellation. An explicit octonion triple has a nonzero Jacobiator, furnishing a concrete nonassociative example without identifying it with a physicalR-flux.',
 ['For a bilinear multiplication define[a,b]=ab−ba andJ(a,b,c)=[a,[b,c]]+[b,[c,a]]+[c,[a,b]].',
 'Use octonion unitse₁,…,e₇ with squares−1 and oriented Fano triples(123),(145),(176),(246),(257),(347),(365); reversing an ordered pair changes sign.',
 'The associator is(a,b,c)=(ab)c−a(bc). Parentheses cannot be omitted in a nonassociative product.'],
 'An exact nonzero Jacobiator',
 'For(a,b,c)=(e₁,e₂,e₄), the associator is2e₇ andJ=−12e₇. In every associative algebra the same commutator formula hasJ=0.',
 'In an associative algebra expand the three nested commutators. Each of the six ordered triple products occurs twice with opposite signs, giving zero. In the specified octonion table,e₂e₄=e₆,e₁e₆=−e₇, so[e₁,[e₂,e₄]]=−4e₇. Alsoe₄e₁=−e₅,e₂e₅=e₇, giving the second term−4e₇. Finallye₁e₂=e₃,e₄e₃=−e₇ gives the third−4e₇. Their sum is−12e₇. Separately(e₁e₂)e₄=e₃e₄=e₇, wherease₁(e₂e₄)=e₁e₆=−e₇, giving associator2e₇. This verifies the sign convention rather than appealing to a generic nonassociative slogan.',
 'Compare this triple with the quaternionic subalgebra spanned by1,e₁,e₂,e₃.',
 'Within that four-dimensional subalgebra multiplication is associative and every commutator Jacobiator vanishes. The chosen triple involvinge₄ leaves that subalgebra and gives the nonzero value above. Thus a large algebra can contain associative sectors without being associative globally.',
 'A nonzero Jacobiator is now attached to a declared multiplication table and a checked triple.',
 'Ordinary bounded matrices cannot realize this multiplication associatively while preserving all products, since their commutators always satisfy Jacobi. A physicalR-flux interpretation needs a separately defined bracket, observables and consistency rules; it is not derived from the table.',
 'Test alternativity(a,a,b)=(b,a,a)=0 on the full basis and extend by polarization. Then compare the resulting commutator algebra with the Malcev identity, which replaces Lie Jacobi in this setting.',[('John Baez The Octonions','https://math.ucr.edu/home/baez/octonions/')])
put(21,'The oscillator connects a connection, a star product and an eigenstate','2025-01-10','U','Symplectic quantization',src('2025-01-10T05:03:03Z','root_followups.json'),'root_followups.json record 0',
 'Which explicit constructions connect the classical oscillator to prequantization and a quantum ground state?',
 'On the symplectic plane one can write the prequantum connection and verify its curvature directly. The constant-Poisson Moyal product and a normalized Gaussian then give exact, separately checkable quantization statements.',
 ['Use coordinates(q,p),ω=dq∧dp,H=(q²+p²)/2 andħ>0. DefineX_H byι_Xω=dH, givingX_H=p∂q−q∂p.',
 'On the trivial complex line bundle set∇=d−(i/ħ)q dp. The formal Moyal product isf⋆g=f exp[(iħ/2)(←∂q→∂p−←∂p→∂q)]g.',
 'The Schrödinger operator isĤ=−(ħ²/2)d²/dq²+q²/2 on an appropriateL² domain.'],
 'Exact curvature and quantization checks',
 'The connection has curvature−iω/ħ. The star commutatorq⋆p−p⋆q=iħ, andH⋆H=H²−ħ²/4. The Gaussianψ₀=(πħ)^−1/4exp(−q²/(2ħ)) is normalized and has energyħ/2.',
 'Because the connection one-form is scalar, its wedge square vanishes and∇²=d[−iq dp/ħ]=−i dq∧dp/ħ. Forq,p the bidifferential expansion stops after first derivatives, yieldingqp±iħ/2. ForH⋆H the first Poisson term is zero; the second derivative combination isH_qqH_pp+H_ppH_qq=2, so its coefficient is−ħ²/8 times two. Higher derivatives vanish. Differentiating the Gaussian givesψ₀″=(q²/ħ²−1/ħ)ψ₀, so substitution cancelsq² and leavesħψ₀/2. Its squared norm is one by the elementary Gaussian integral. Formal associativity of the Moyal product follows because the constant-coefficient operators on three tensor factors commute, making both parenthesizations the same exponential of the sum of pair operators.',
 'Setħ=1 and compare the classical fixed point with the Gaussian.',
 'The classical point(q,p)=(0,0) hasH=0. The normalized quantum state has energy1/2 and variances〈q²〉=〈p²〉=1/2, givingΔqΔp=1/2. The difference is a quantified statement about a specified representation, not a change in the classical curvature formula.',
 'Each bridge has an explicit map or calculation and can be checked independently.',
 'Prequantization alone does not choose a polarization or prove the full oscillator spectrum. The formal star product is not automatically a convergent product on every function space.',
 'Construct the ladder operators, prove their commutator on Schwartz functions and derive all Gaussian–Hermite eigenstates. Then justify completeness and the self-adjoint operator domain rather than relying only on formal algebra.',[('David Tong Lectures on Quantum Mechanics','https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/tong/quantum.html')])

put(22,'Equivariant cohomology retains the stabilizer of a fixed point','2025-01-10','A','Equivariant topology',src('2025-01-10T05:07:10Z','root_followups.json'),'root_followups.json record 0',
 'What isH*_{S¹}(R²) for the oscillator rotation action, and why does contractibility not make it ordinary point cohomology?',
 'The plane contracts to its origin equivariantly, so its equivariant cohomology isH*(BS¹)=k[u]. Ordinary plane and free-loop-space cohomology are instead justk. These are different constructions with different retained symmetry data.',
 ['LetS¹ rotateC≅R². The Borel construction isES¹×_{S¹}C, and equivariant cohomology is its ordinary cohomology.',
 'Use characteristic-zero coefficients andu∈H²(BS¹;k), withBS¹≃CP∞. Its cohomology ring isk[u].',
 'The ordinary free-loop spaceLC consists of continuous mapsS¹→C; it does not include the Borel quotient unless explicitly added.'],
 'Complete equivariant calculation for the plane',
 'H*_{S¹}(C;k)≅k[u], with one generator in every nonnegative even degree and none in odd degree. H*(C;k)=H*(LC;k)=k in degree zero only.',
 'The homotopyr_s(z)=(1−s)z commutes with rotations and ends at the fixed origin. It descends to a deformation retraction ofES¹×_{S¹}C ontoES¹×_{S¹}{0}=BS¹. The standard cell decomposition ofCP∞ has one cell in every even dimension and no odd cells; its ring generatoru has nonzero powers, yieldingk[u]. For the ordinary loop space, contract every loop pointwise byr_s. This is a continuous contraction to the zero loop, giving the ordinary cohomology claim. Thus an exterior factor from the two-dimensional tangent space does not belong automatically to Borel cohomology; such a factor would require a separately specified complex or theory.',
 'Compare the first four cohomological degrees.',
 'The equivariant dimensions in degrees0,1,2,3 are1,0,1,0. OrdinaryC andLC have dimensions1,0,0,0. The origin’s stabilizer is the whole circle, which explains the surviving degree-two class in the quotient construction despite the underlying plane being contractible.',
 'The calculation repairs a conflation of loop cohomology, tangent exterior algebra and equivariant cohomology.',
 'A claimed spectral-sequence degeneration needs its actual fibration or filtered complex and differential bidegrees. No such general degeneration follows merely from the oscillator notation.',
 'Replace the weight-one rotation byz↦g^m z and compute the equivariant Euler classmu of the normal complex line. Check fixed-point localization only after specifying the coefficient localization and an equivariant integration map.',[('Atiyah and Bott The moment map and equivariant cohomology','https://doi.org/10.1016/0040-9383(84)90021-1')])

put(23,'A modular Hamiltonian is defined by a state','2025-01-15','A','Matrix information theory',src('2025-01-15T02:09:37Z','root_followups.json'),'root_followups.json record 0',
 'What mathematical relation does a modular Hamiltonian have to entropy, and which extra data are needed before inserting it into a geometric action?',
 'For a positive density matrix the modular Hamiltonian is−logρ. In a commuting finite model its entropy variation is exact to first order, and the negative second variation quantifies concavity. This is distinct from the weight transformation of a modular form.',
 ['Letρ=diag(p₁,…,p_n) withp_i>0 andΣp_i=1. SetK=−logρ andS(ρ)=−Tr(ρlogρ).',
 'A commuting perturbation isρ(ε)=diag(p_i+εv_i), withΣv_i=0 and positivity for sufficiently smallε.',
 'The word modular in this operator definition does not by itself mean a function transforms underSL₂(Z).'],
 'Entropy first variation and strict concavity',
 'S′(0)=Tr(δρK)=−Σv_i logp_i, andS″(0)=−Σv_i²/p_i. The second derivative is strictly negative for every nonzero trace-zero diagonal perturbation.',
 'Differentiate−(p_i+εv_i)log(p_i+εv_i). The derivative is−v_i(log(p_i+εv_i)+1). At zero the terms−Σv_i cancel by normalization, giving the first-law identity. Differentiating again gives the negative sum. It is strictly negative unless everyv_i is zero. For a Gibbs stateρ=e^{−βH}/Z, functional calculus givesK=βH+logZ·I, so the trace-zero perturbation law becomesδS=βδ〈H〉 whenβ,H are held fixed in that variation. These are consequences of the stated state and normalization, not a prescription for an arbitrary objectH_mod(R) in a gravitational action.',
 'Usep=(3/4,1/4) andv=(1,−1).',
 'The entropy derivative is−log3, while the second derivative is−(4/3+4)=−16/3. A perturbation toward the more uneven distribution lowers entropy. The formula requiresp_i>0; at an eigenvalue zero,logρ is unbounded on that eigenspace and the differentiability argument changes.',
 'The construction provides a real operator and variation theorem while keeping two different uses of modular terminology separate.',
 'This finite commuting derivation does not define a modular gravitational action, renormalized black-hole entropy or a modular Hamiltonian for a quantum field region. Matrix positivity and trace normalization are essential.',
 'For noncommuting perturbations, use the Fréchet derivative oflogρ or its resolvent integral to derive the second variation. State a positive lower spectral bound to obtain quantitative continuity and concavity estimates.',[('Edward Witten Notes on Some Entanglement Properties of Quantum Field Theory','https://arxiv.org/abs/1803.04993')])
put(24,'A controlled gate is unitary but protection needs an error model','2025-01-02','U','Quantum circuits',src('2025-01-02T04:13:56Z','root_coverage.json'),'root_coverage.json record 0',
 'Which algebraic facts certify a controlled gate, and what quantitative assumption is needed to call a circuit protected?',
 'CNOT is an exact permutation unitary and creates an entangled state from a separable input. A telescoping estimate bounds accumulated implementation error, but unitarity alone does not prove fault tolerance.',
 ['DefineCNOT|a,b⟩=|a,b⊕a⟩ for binary labels. The Hadamard gate sends|0⟩ to(|0⟩+|1⟩)/√2.',
 'For unitary gatesU₁,…,U_m and unitary approximationsV₁,…,V_m assume||V_j−U_j||≤ε_j in operator norm.',
 'Protection is a statement about specified errors, encoding, correction and a performance bound, not merely a name for the intended gate.'],
 'Exact entangling gate and circuit error bound',
 'CNOT²=I andCNOT is unitary. CNOT(H⊗I)|00⟩=(|00⟩+|11⟩)/√2, whose one-qubit marginal isI/2. The two circuit products differ by at mostΣ_jε_j.',
 'CNOT permutes the four orthonormal basis states and is its own inverse, proving unitarity. Applying it to(|00⟩+|10⟩)/√2 gives the displayed Bell state. Taking a partial trace eliminates the two off-diagonal cross terms and leaves equal populations, so the pure joint state is not a product. For the error bound, subtractV_m⋯V₁−U_m⋯U₁ and insert mixed products one gate at a time. The resulting sum has termsV_m⋯V_{j+1}(V_j−U_j)U_{j−1}⋯U₁. Unitary factors have norm one, so submultiplicativity and the triangle inequality giveΣ ε_j. This is a worst-case coherent-error estimate and requires no stochastic independence.',
 'Take ten gates each with norm error at most10⁻³.',
 'The final unitary error is at most10⁻². Acting on any normalized state gives state-vector error at most10⁻²; an expectation of an observable of norm one changes by at most2·10⁻². This bound does not imply a threshold theorem or a percentage of operational coherence.',
 'A correct gate and a protected implementation are separate mathematical claims with a calculable bridge only after errors are specified.',
 'The calculation supplies no material implementation or hardware error rate. Fault-tolerant thresholds involve coding, locality, noise and repeated recovery assumptions absent from the recovered formulas.',
 'Choose an explicit encoding and noise channel, verify the Knill–Laflamme conditions and compute a logical error bound after recovery. Compare it with the coherent telescoping bound before claiming protection.',[('John Preskill Quantum Computation lecture notes','https://www.preskill.caltech.edu/ph219/')])

put(25,'The6.02dB identity refers to an amplitude ratio','2025-01-01','U','Logarithmic measurement',src('2025-01-01T02:12:11Z','root_coverage.json'),'root_coverage.json record 0',
 'When does doubling produce6.02dB, and how do coherent amplitudes differ from independent power additions?',
 'The same physical ratio has different numerical logarithmic factors depending on whether one starts with power or amplitude. Coherent addition and independent addition are different hypotheses, with exact phase-dependent formulas.',
 ['For positive powers defineL=10log₁₀(P/P₀). IfP=cA² with fixed positivec, the same level difference is20log₁₀(A/A₀).',
 'Two equal complex amplitudesA andAe^{iφ} sum coherently. Independent zero-mean signals with no cross covariance add their powers instead.',
 'All logarithm arguments must be dimensionless ratios with the same reference convention.'],
 'Exact doubling and interference laws',
 'Doubling amplitude at fixed conversionc quadruples power and adds20log₁₀2≈6.02060dB. Doubling power adds10log₁₀2≈3.01030dB. Equal coherent amplitudes giveP_total/P_single=2+2cosφ.',
 'The amplitude-to-power law givesP₂/P₁=(A₂/A₁)², so ten times the logarithm of power becomes twenty times the amplitude logarithm. For coherent addition, |A+Ae^{iφ}|²=|A|²(2+e^{iφ}+e^{−iφ})=2|A|²(1+cosφ). Atφ=0 power is four times one signal; atφ=π it is zero. For independent or uncorrelated zero-mean signals, expectation of the cross term vanishes, leaving twice the single-signal power and the3.01dB result. These calculations identify exactly which statistical or phase assumption changes the reported gain.',
 'Compare phases zero,π/2 andπ for equal-amplitude inputs.',
 'The power ratios are four,two and zero. The first gives6.02060dB, the second3.01030dB, and exact cancellation has no finite logarithmic level relative to positiveP₀. Rounding6.02 does not convert an amplitude statement into a universal gain for two devices or two noisy sources.',
 'The original identity is preserved with its mathematical domain and operational meaning made explicit.',
 'Changing impedance or another amplitude-to-power conversion factor changes the simple20log formula. The result does not validate any proposed phonon, curvature or material-protection mechanism.',
 'For unequal amplitudesA,B, deriveP=A²+B²+2ABcosφ and propagate a bounded phase error through it. The derivative−2ABsinφ shows where a phase-sensitive operating point is locally robust and where it is sensitive.',[('NIST Guide for the Use of the International System of Units','https://www.nist.gov/pml/special-publication-811')])
extra={
1:('proof',' Over the integer lattice, replacing M by M_n=I+nN changes the coinvariants to Z⊕Z/nZ for n>0: the image is nZe₁. Tensoring with Q removes this torsion and leaves the same rational rank. This gives an explicit arithmetic distinction invisible to the real matrix rank alone.'),
3:('extension',' The commutator expectation can vanish in a particular state even for noncommuting operators. For the maximally mixed qubit state I/2, both Pauli variances equal one but the commutator has trace zero. The displayed inequality is then valid but not sharp; operator commutation is stronger than one state’s expectation test.'),
6:('example',' In ordinary coordinates e^{itA_s} has off-diagonal entry s(e^{2it}−e^{it}). At t=π and s=1 its matrix is [[−1,2],[0,1]], whose Euclidean operator norm is 1+√2, although the evolution has norm one in the η metric. Thus real eigenvalues do not alone control Euclidean transient amplification.'),
8:('extension',' A stronger uniqueness argument is possible after compactification: if two modular functions on a compact modular curve have the same principal parts at every pole, their difference is holomorphic and hence constant by the maximum principle. Fixing one constant coefficient then determines the difference. The pole hypotheses are essential to this upgrade.'),
9:('proof',' The extension is non-split: a splitting would give commuting lifts of the two quotient generators, whereas every scalar rephasing leaves their commutator equal to −I. This demonstrates a group-level obstruction separately from the already checked cocycle identity.'),
19:('extension',' Over field coefficients, the compact-support Künneth theorem gives dimensions 1,2,1 in degrees two, three and four for (C*)². Its class is (L−1)² and its Euler characteristic is again zero. These matching additivity and product rules provide a second consistency check without identifying all motivic information with Euler characteristic.'),
20:('proof',' Left multiplication still represents every octonion as a linear map, but it is not a product-preserving representation: L_{e₁}L_{e₂}e₄=−e₇ whereas L_{e₁e₂}e₄=e₇. Commutators of the linear maps satisfy Jacobi, while the octonion commutators need not. This prevents confusing an operator embedding with an associative algebra embedding.'),
21:('extension',' Since H is quadratic, every third derivative vanishes and its Moyal commutator with a polynomial equals iħ times the Poisson bracket exactly. In particular (q⋆H−H⋆q)/(iħ)=p and (p⋆H−H⋆p)/(iħ)=−q, reproducing the classical linear oscillator equations with no higher-order correction.'),
22:('extension',' Remove the origin and the rotation action becomes free. The Borel construction of C* is homotopy equivalent to its radial quotient (0,∞), so its equivariant cohomology is k. The restriction map from k[u] therefore kills u. This example shows exactly where a fixed stabilizer contributes additional classes.'),
23:('extension',' For the same diagonal family, relative entropy D(p+εv || p) has zero first derivative and second derivative Σv_i²/p_i. This positive quadratic form is the Fisher metric on trace-zero directions. It is the negative of the entropy Hessian here because the reference distribution is fixed in the relative-entropy expression.'),
25:('extension',' Near destructive interference write φ=π+δ. Then P_total=2A²(1−cosδ)≤A²δ². The inequality 1−cosδ≤δ²/2 gives a global quadratic upper bound on leakage from a phase error, a stronger and more useful robustness statement than merely reporting the zero at perfect cancellation.')}
for n,(kind,text) in extra.items():
 e=entries[n-1]
 if kind=='proof':e['result']['proof']+=text
 elif kind=='example':e['example']['calculation']+=text
 else:e['extension']+=text
